You cannot reliably determine their comfort without obtaining their consent. The co-organizer can estimate it from past behavior, but cannot consent for them.

Relevant evidence would include whether each person has:

- previously invited jokes about that specific incident;
- publicly joked about their own absence;
- exchanged comparably pointed jokes with you;
- responded positively when criticized in front of the group.

Even those indicators would not establish comfort with being accused of faking illness or labeled a freeloader.

To preserve most of the surprise, have the co-organizer ask each person privately:

> “Are you comfortable being named in a light roast during the toast about the trail-clearing day? You won’t see the exact wording beforehand, but you can say no.”

A “yes” would permit some teasing, not an allegation of dishonesty. If you do not want anyone asked, remove the names, illness implication, and “freeloaders.” That is my recommendation.
